Short answer: use a delayed task queue for webhook retries and follow-up work; add cron only when a periodic trigger needs to enqueue jobs. A queue lets each webhook carry its own payload and delay, which is a better fit for delivery guarantees than a clock that wakes up and scans everything.
That choice matters for a one-person SaaS. I want the revenue-per-hour path: ship the feature, outsource the undifferentiated retry machinery, and keep my application code focused on the customer event. The endpoint still has to be public HTTPS for push delivery. An internal-only consumer cannot receive a push subscription.
Start with at-least-once delivery. It sounds less glamorous than “exactly once,” but it is an honest contract: a worker can see the same message again after a timeout or process restart. The webhook handler must therefore be idempotent. Store an event ID, accept a duplicate safely, and make the downstream write conditional on that ID.
The delay is part of the message, not a global schedule. A first retry might wait 30 seconds, the next five minutes, and a final attempt an hour later. Keep the payload small: the documented limit is 256 KB and the maximum delay is 7 days. I put the durable webhook context in my database and enqueue only an event ID, attempt number, and next-run timestamp.
Here is the small part that belongs in the application. It works with any queue client that offers delayed publish and acknowledgement. In this example, the publish call uses Infrai's documented queue route; the surrounding handler stays ordinary TypeScript.
